      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "There is no single universally agreed-upon ethical answer, as the debate hinges on conflicting principles of cultural sovereignty versus global heritage preservation. While many modern curators argue that universal museums provide unparalleled conservation expertise and accessibility, critics rightly point out that this argument often justifies the continued holding of looted or stolen items. Consequently, the most ethically sound position today likely lies between the two: prioritizing provenance research and voluntary collaboration with source nations rather than adopting an absolute doctrine of either permanent retention or immediate return. This nuanced approach allows institutions to address historical harm while still leveraging their unique role in safeguarding human history for a global audience.",
